From 0c87766499819101bfaef2d9b218fc9eb800f77e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bruce Ashfield <bruce.ashfield@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 08:14:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] vxn: tear down the DomU on destroy/timeout; fix
 unenforced policy & env leak

Address the review findings on PR #38, reworked after config-b testing showed
the first cut left the DomU running.

P1:
- destroy() tears down the exact DomU. vxn derives the domain name
  "vxn-<name>" from `vxn run --name` (paired meta-virt build_runner_args
  change), so the backend `xl destroy`es that domain (with a `vxn rm`
  fallback) instead of only killing the blocked `xl create -c` frontend,
  which orphaned the VM.
- axis-cli calls destroy() after wait() on the non-interactive run too, so a
  timeout (which only kills the frontend) still tears the DomU down.
- the frontend pid is retained so destroy() can signal it after wait() has
  moved the Child into a blocking task (interactive SIGTERM path).
- new() rejects process.run_as_user / non-empty process.blocked_syscalls with
  Unsupported rather than silently weakening the requested policy.
- per-run env moves off the vxn argv into the child environment (VXN_ENV_B64)
  so secrets are not exposed on the world-readable cmdline.

P2:
- env records are validated before transport: a key with '='/newline or a
  value with a newline is rejected (Unsupported) rather than mis-parsed as
  extra assignments in the newline-delimited KEY=VAL blob.
- the filesystem filter skips only real placeholder tokens ({workspace}/
  {tmpdir}); a concrete path with literal braces (e.g. /opt/data{old}) is
  still emitted, and under filesystem.compatibility=hard_requirement an
  unmappable required rule is rejected instead of silently dropped.

